<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles III, Prince of Monaco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a>–<a href="/years/1889">1889</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles III, Prince of Monaco, ruled from 1856 to 1889. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menton">Menton</a> and Roquebrune broke away in 1848, he sold Monaco's claims to them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1861, leaving only the small principality. To replace the lost revenue he founded the casino at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-carlo">Monte Carlo</a>, named after him, which made Monaco prosperous.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-iii-prince-of-monaco.jpg" alt="Charles III, Prince of Monaco" width="320" height="529" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_III_(Monaco).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Today Canada is a federation of ten provinces and three territories with about 41 million people, the second-largest country in the world by area, and a constitutional monarchy under King <b>Charles III</b>, with an economy deeply integrated with that of the United States.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Charles III</b> was assassinated in Parma in 1854, leaving the regent Louise Marie of Artois to govern for the young Robert I. During the Second Italian War of Independence the regent left in June 1859, provisional authorities, later led by Luigi Carlo Farini, joined the duchy…</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke <b>Charles III</b> was left with a few fortresses.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1861 Prince <b>Charles III</b> of Monaco sold his rights to Napoleon III's Second French Empire for four million francs.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-cities-of-menton-and-roquebrune">Free Cities of Menton and Roquebrune</a></li></ul></section>
